cc65 reproducible debug info — deterministic, location/time-independent objects.

WHY: With -g, cc65/ca65 bake three environment-dependent values into every
object file's debug info: (1) an OPT_DATETIME wall-clock timestamp stamped by
ca65 at assembly time, (2) each source file's modification time, and (3) each
source file's full path. None affect generated code — a debugger uses them only
to locate a source and check whether it changed — but they make the object
depend on WHEN and WHERE it was built. That breaks byte-for-byte reproducibility.

It bites us concretely: romdev ships cc65 both as a native binary and as a WASM
build. The WASM build stages inputs into a virtual filesystem under "/work/...",
each written with a single mtime, so assembling the very same source through the
WASM ca65 produces a different .o than the native ca65 — different debug paths,
different mtimes, different OPT_DATETIME. Downstream, the GameTank SDK's
FLASH2M bank-placement measures object/segment sizes, so this cosmetic delta
perturbs code layout and can flip a tight build between fitting and overflowing.

FIX (all in the debug-info emission paths; generated code is untouched):
  - ca65 OPT_DATETIME: use SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH if set, else 0 (not wall clock).
  - ca65 FILE table: intern the basename (not the full path) and pin the mtime
    to SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH-or-0. GetFileIndex basenames to match, so .dbg line
    references still resolve. --create-dep is unaffected in practice (romdev
    never uses it) but note it now emits basenames.
  - cc65 ".dbg file"/".dbg line": emit the basename and a pinned mtime so the
    generated assembly itself is path/time independent (this is what makes the
    .s byte-identical, which in turn makes ca65's embedded .s size match).
  - A shared common/filetime.c:GetSourceDateEpoch() honors SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
    (the cross-toolchain reproducible-build convention).

SENTINEL for idempotent re-apply: GetSourceDateEpoch in src/common/filetime.c.

